"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (and §{#} for the X-ray lines). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Using the demo.</strong> This worksheet uses the X-rays view of the Electron Diffraction demo (the X-rays button above the picture).",
"An X-ray tube run at {#} kV, with a copper or a molybdenum anode, sends a narrow beam onto a crystal of sodium chloride (NaCl) or lithium fluoride (LiF) on a turntable. The crystal is cut so that its face is parallel to its ({#}) planes.",
"You turn the crystal to a glancing angle \\(\\theta\\) between {#}° and {#}°. The counter rides on an arm at \\(2\\theta\\), so it always sits where a ray reflected from the planes would go. It shows the count rate in counts per second.",
"The graph traces the count rate against \\(\\theta\\) as you turn the crystal. Scan turns it from {#}° to {#}° on its own; Clear the graph starts a new trace.",
"Record reading stores the tube, the crystal, \\(\\theta\\) and the count rate. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> The peaks are at the exact Bragg angles of the Kα₁ and Kβ₁ lines of copper ({#} nm and {#} nm) and molybdenum ({#} nm and {#} nm), from the line energies of the X-ray Data Booklet, reflected by the ({#}) planes of NaCl and LiF in every order that fits. The heights of the peaks, their width ({#}°), how they fall with the order, the smooth background from the continuous spectrum and the steady background of {#} counts per second are illustrative, chosen to look like a teaching spectrometer. The weaker lines (Kα₂ among them) are left out, and the count rate shown is the expected rate; a real counter’s counts scatter by about their square root. Simulated data.",
"Use a crystal as a diffraction grating for X-rays: measure the angles at which the characteristic lines of an X-ray tube are reflected, find their wavelengths from a crystal of known spacing, and then use them to measure the spacing of another crystal.",
"X-rays are electromagnetic waves with wavelengths around {#} to {#} nm, about the spacing of atoms in a solid. In a crystal, the atoms lie on many sets of parallel planes. A wave reflected from one plane adds to the waves reflected from the planes below it only if the extra path, \\(2d\\sin\\theta\\), is a whole number of wavelengths (§{#}):",
"\\[2d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda, \\qquad m = 1, 2, 3, \\ldots\\]",
"This is Bragg’s law. Here \\(d\\) is the spacing of the planes and \\(\\theta\\) the glancing angle between the beam and the planes, not the angle from the normal. The reflected beam leaves at \\(2\\theta\\) to the incident beam. For a fixed \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(d\\), \\(\\sin\\theta\\) is in proportion to the order \\(m\\).",
"An X-ray tube gives a smooth spread of wavelengths (it starts at the shortest wavelength \\(hc/eV\\)) and, on top of it, sharp lines that are the anode metal’s own: the stronger Kα line and the weaker, shorter Kβ line (§{#}). Each line is reflected at its own angle in each order, so a scan of \\(\\theta\\) shows pairs of peaks, Kβ at the smaller angle.",
"The ({#}) planes of NaCl are \\(d = 0.2820\\) nm apart.",
"Copper Kα X-rays have \\(\\lambda = 0.15406\\) nm. At what angles \\(\\theta\\) should they be reflected from NaCl’s ({#}) planes, in each order? What is the highest order that can be seen, and why is there a highest one?",
